[Music]  hello everyone I'm Alicia Malone welcome  to the very first episode of fandango's  indie movie guide each week I'll be  giving you my picks about what to watch  in theaters at home which movies are the  most buzzed-about at film festivals and  what to look out for [ __ ] awards season I  promise I will tell it to you straight  and in true indie spirit I am hosting  editing producing writing and shooting  this show myself this week I'm at the  Toronto International Film Festival  which is one of the biggest and best  film festivals of the year it's right  here that lots of that award season buzz  gets created so I thought I'd give you a  list of the 5 best movies I've seen here  at TIFF let's start with my favorite  Manchester by the sea this is a film  you'll definitely hear about come award  season hopefully for Casey Affleck he  really deserves Best Actor for this he  plays a man battling with demons from  his past he is forced to face those  demons when his brother dies and he has  to take guardianship over his nephew I  think the idea was that you would  relocate relocate to Whitwell if you dig  it was my impression that you'd spent a  lot of time here I'm just a backup  nobody can appreciate what you've been  through and if you really feel you can't  take this on me that's your right I  don't want to spoil exactly what  happened to him in the past because  director Kenneth Lonnegan allows the  audience to piece things together slowly  and when you finally put all the pieces  of that jigsaw puzzle together it will  be like an emotional hit to your heart  it floored me this movie KC is excellent  also Michelle Williams is brilliant too  moonlight is a lovely film directed by  Barry Jenkins it tells the story of a  young man called sharone who lives in  Miami and the story is told in three  parts one when he's a boy one when he's  a teenager and one when he's an older  man  Tyrone has a lot to deal with including  a crack addict mother played by Naomi  Harris and also his own battle with his  sexuality  this Tahoma huh to you it's very subtle  very gently told and it all feels like  kind of a meditation on masculinity  definitely look out for moonlight  arrival is an intelligent sci-fi by  directed to me Villeneuve he's the same  guy that did prisoners and Sicario but  this one feels completely different it  stars Amy Adams as a linguistics  professor who is charged with speaking  with aliens when they arrive at 12  different places on earth and jeremy  renner is the scientist who's working  next to her  not everyone is wired for what you're  about to do it's where they look like  you'll see soon enough  every 18 hours this movie does not go  where you might expect it does have a  little bit of Spielberg feel it's a  little bit of Kubrick but a lot more  Christopher Nolan and Terrence Malick  than I expected  it maybe a little bit out there for some  but if you go along with the ride and  open your mind a little bit then you'll  have a great time  la-la-land is by director Damien  Chazelle and he's the same guy who is  behind whiplash similar to that film  this one also deals with ambition and  jazz music but the tone is completely  different this stars Ryan Gosling and  Emma Stone in their third collaboration  together and they have wonderful  chemistry he's an aspiring jazz musician  she's an aspiring actress and they're  both trying to make it together and  apart in the crazy City of LA there's so  much that I can't see  it is just so joyful and so much fun to  watch the production design is wonderful  I wanted to watch this movie again I  wanted to study it frame by frame I  wanted to live inside it and finally  lion starring Dev Patel the movie that  got me right in the field  [Music]  I have to find my way back home this is  based on a true story about a young  Indian boy called Sioux who lost his  family in Calcutta and ended up being  adopted by an Australian family played  by the always brilliant Nicole Kidman  and the equally excellent David Wenham  years later the grown ups Arun now  played by Dev Patel becomes obsessed  with the idea of trying to find his  family which of course is kind of like  finding a needle in a haystack  Rooney Mara also stars as soos  girlfriend  I loved this movie it is so emotional  and just gorgeous the way that it's all  put together the way that it comes  together  directed by Garth Davis and Dev Patel is  wonderful special shout out also to Tom  Ford's nocturnal animals which is really  trippy but interesting and also Jay a by  owners a monster Paul's  which will definitely make you cry  and that's it for the first episode of  fandango's indie movie guide I hope you  enjoyed it let me know in the comments  below which movies you're the most  excited about seeing and you can check  to me anytime on twitter at Alicia  Malone I'll see you soon  [Music]

